WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:06.560
Well, hello and
welcome everyone.

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:08.720
I'm Debbie Douglas, for
those of you that don't know,

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:11.040
I'm the curator of
science and technology

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:13.680
and the director of
collections at the MIT Museum.

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:18.560
And gathered with me on this
lovely day in October 2021

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:23.760
are the museum's four
directors, distinguished members

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:27.040
of our advisory board,
and my many hardworking

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:29.920
and talented colleagues
on the staff.

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:33.520
Today's session is being
videotaped for posterity

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:36.720
so we'd be grateful if you
would silence your phones

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:41.840
and be relatively quiet.

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:47.520
Although, we'll welcome
all bouquets and cheers

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:51.520
that you might be
inclined to share

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:54.160
with our distinguished speakers.

00:00:54.160 --> 00:01:00.800
Today is the final program to be
held in the MIT Museum facility

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:02.960
at 265 Massachusetts Avenue.

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:09.040
I want to begin with MIT's land
use acknowledgment statement.

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:12.720
MIT acknowledges
Indigenous peoples

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:15.040
as the traditional
stewards of this land

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:16.880
and the enduring
relationship that

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:20.160
exists between them and their
traditional territories.

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:24.720
The land on which we sit is the
traditional unceded territory

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:28.560
of the Wampanoag Nation and we
acknowledge the painful history

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:32.000
of genocide and forced
occupation of their territory.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:35.280
And we honor and respect the
many diverse Indigenous peoples

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:40.320
connected to this land on which
we gather from time immemorial.

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:43.760
It is good for us to
remember that our 50 year

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:47.120
relationship on this site
is only the most recent

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:50.000
and that the history of
this place is much older.

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:54.320
So we have here four people.

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:57.760
Some of you know them all, some
of you know one or two of them.

00:01:58.480 --> 00:01:59.520
Warren Seamans,

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:03.840
Warren is the founding
director of the MIT Museum

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:07.280
but his tenure at
MIT predates that.

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:10.240
He was in the Air
Force even earlier.

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:16.880
The Museum of course,
his tenure with what

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:19.840
we call the MIT
Museum, began in 1971

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:23.040
and continued to his
retirement in 1996.

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:28.800
He was passionate about
history, arts, culture, and MIT

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:31.680
and he has
entrepreneurial instincts

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:35.920
that quickly apprehended
something that MIT in 1971

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:38.560
had not fully
known about itself,

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:41.680
and that's secretly,
at MIT loves history.

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:47.040
In 1994 Warren had
appointed Mary Lean

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:49.600
as associate director.

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:52.000
It was a new post, I
believe at the time.

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:54.800
The museum was
growing and it needed

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:58.320
to focus its operations,
finances, and staffing

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:00.640
to professionalize them.

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:02.960
And over nearly
three decades Mary

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:05.440
has built the operational
infrastructure

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:09.440
that has enabled the flourishing
of every single person,

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:13.600
program, exhibition, fundraising
effort, expansion, and more

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:16.000
that has been dreamed
up since her arrival.

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:19.600
But twice, Mary has
also served as acting

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:24.800
director between 1996
and 1998 and then again

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:27.440
between 2002 and 2005.

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:31.600
And Jane Pickering
was appointed in 1998

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:35.120
and served a four eventful
and transformative years

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:40.000
in the history of this
museum before departing

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:41.680
for Yale's Peabody Museum.

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:44.960
She subsequently returned
to Cambridge in 2013

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:49.040
as the inaugural director
of the Harvard Museums

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:51.600
of Science and Culture
and more recently,

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:54.400
in her new position
as the William

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:57.040
and Muriel Seabury
Howell's director

00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:00.240
of Harvard's Peabody Museum
of Archaeology and Ethnology.

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:08.480
Allen Brody, who was
Philip Corey's predecessor,

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:10.880
the associate
provost for the Arts.

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:16.560
Once described John,
your arrival in 2005 not

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:19.600
so much as an appointment
but that rather

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:21.520
you had been shot
out of a cannon

00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:23.040
into the Institute's midst.

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:28.080
MIT had come to
recognize and value

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:30.720
the achievements of
Warren, Jane, and Mary

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:32.560
all the more
exceptional considering

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:36.320
the limited finances, facility,
and staffing that they had.

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:38.000
But could it scale?

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:40.480
That was the question and
the challenge for John

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:43.440
who came to us as one of
the world's leading scholars

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:45.680
on the public
engagement of science

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:48.320
and from leadership positions
at prestigious posts

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:49.280
throughout the UK.

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:53.680
The question was could he
increase the visibility

00:04:53.680 --> 00:04:58.480
and impact of the museum by two
or three orders of magnitude?

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:01.920
But Warren I wanted
to ask about--

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:04.400
begin by asking about
the museum's early years.

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:10.480
And I want to pause for a moment
and set the stage for all of us

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:14.400
here tonight and bring
you back to 1971.

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:18.000
When gas was $0.36
a gallon, when

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:21.760
Walt Disney World in Florida
had opened for the first time,

00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:25.200
when NASA was launching
its final Apollo missions,

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:28.320
when the ARPANET debut, when
Ali fought Frazier, when

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:31.040
the Temptations sang,
"Justy My Imagination",

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:35.520
cigarette advertising on
TV had just been banned,

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:37.920
the voting age
was lowered to 18,

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:40.080
and Americans were
painfully divided

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:42.480
over virtually every
issue of consequence

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:45.040
from race and gender, gay
rights, the environment,

00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:48.080
housing, education,
jobs, and most of all

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:50.000
the conflict in Southeast Asia.

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:54.640
So given all that
upheaval and discord

00:05:54.640 --> 00:05:58.320
of that moment 50
years ago, when

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:02.480
plans are being made
for the October 7th 1971

00:06:02.480 --> 00:06:06.240
inauguration of Jerome
Wiesner, the theme

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:08.960
of that inauguration,
of a 10 day event,

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:10.560
was called introspection.

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:16.880
Wiesner sensed that the moment
that called for reflection was,

00:06:16.880 --> 00:06:20.720
at this moment was called for
reflection and re-dedication

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:24.320
of the community to the
values and ideas we all share.

00:06:24.320 --> 00:06:26.560
There was a 10 day
celebration, they've

00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:30.160
never done anything like this
before that featured concerts

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:32.480
and colloquium and
parties and exhibitions.

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:35.520
But today I want
to call attention

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:41.120
to one particular exhibition,
it's called Retrospect MIT 19--

00:06:41.120 --> 00:06:43.920
--or excuse me 1861 to 1916.

00:06:44.720 --> 00:06:46.640
Now the tech reports
that the exhibit

00:06:46.640 --> 00:06:48.720
was organized by
Professor Richard

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:52.640
Douglas, the head of the
Department of Humanities.

00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:55.360
So Warren I want to
have you start off

00:06:55.360 --> 00:06:58.640
this conversation by saying
how did you get involved?

00:06:58.640 --> 00:07:02.240
And how did this small exhibit
of photographs and drawings

00:07:02.240 --> 00:07:05.360
from MIT first 55
years of history

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:07.120
turn into an accredited museum?

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:15.920
OK well very simply, I was in
the Department of Humanities

00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:17.440
as the administrative officer.

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:21.920
And the head of department,
Richard M Douglas,

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:26.000
sent me a note, which is
probably here in the file,

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:29.840
asking me to find a few
photographs to put up

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:33.760
an exhibit for Jerry
Wiesner's inauguration.

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:37.280
So that sounds simple,
I'll just go to archives

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:42.400
and pick up, choose a few
photographs and put them up.

00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:46.960
Well I went to the photographs
and was very quickly informed

00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:48.880
that they didn't
collect photographs.

00:07:49.600 --> 00:07:52.960
That they only collected
the official records

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:55.760
of the Institute and photographs
were not part of that.

00:07:56.560 --> 00:08:00.880
So I started searching and
we started finding things

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:02.240
everywhere we turned around.

00:08:03.200 --> 00:08:05.600
I need to say at
sort of the beginning

00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:09.120
that my previous
job at MIT prior

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:13.600
to the Department of Humanities
was in the personnel or human

00:08:13.600 --> 00:08:14.960
resources that we now call it.

00:08:15.680 --> 00:08:18.880
And in that position
I was in charge

00:08:18.880 --> 00:08:23.600
of finding, hiring,
promoting, whatever you do,

00:08:23.600 --> 00:08:26.160
to the hourly people.

00:08:26.160 --> 00:08:28.240
And there are a lot of
hourly people at MIT.

00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:31.840
So I got to not only a
lot of the hourly people

00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:33.600
but their supervisors as well.

00:08:34.560 --> 00:08:40.880
And they for example,
one of our major

00:08:40.880 --> 00:08:46.480
finds in getting going
was I called on my friend

00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:50.400
Elmer Condon who was
head of the movers.

00:08:51.040 --> 00:08:53.840
And I asked him
if he knew where--

00:08:54.480 --> 00:08:56.080
he knew where everything was.

00:08:56.080 --> 00:09:00.560
Everybody just gave Elmer the
stuff they wanted to get rid of

00:09:00.560 --> 00:09:03.120
and he would find a place,
usually in this building

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:03.520
by the way.

00:09:04.400 --> 00:09:10.400
And the first thing he
led me to was a room up

00:09:10.400 --> 00:09:12.400
on the fourth floor
of this building,

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:16.000
that had not only William
Barton Roger's portrait,

00:09:17.040 --> 00:09:20.800
the original one that no one
had seen for a long, long time,

00:09:21.440 --> 00:09:25.920
but about 20 other important
portraits and busts.

00:09:26.880 --> 00:09:34.240
And I immediately took the
portrait of William Barton

00:09:34.240 --> 00:09:36.800
Rogers down to Howard Johnson.

00:09:37.760 --> 00:09:42.560
And he immediately told
James Rhyne Killian,

00:09:43.360 --> 00:09:45.680
the saint of all saints
as far as I'm concerned.

00:09:46.480 --> 00:09:49.920
And Killian asked to
come up and for me

00:09:49.920 --> 00:09:51.600
to bring him up here to see it.

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:53.600
And he said, don't
let any of this go,

00:09:53.600 --> 00:09:55.760
we got to save the
history of the place.

00:09:55.760 --> 00:09:58.080
So that's basically
the beginning.

00:09:58.080 --> 00:10:01.680
So we started gathering
and physically searching

00:10:01.680 --> 00:10:03.440
for whatever we could find.

00:10:03.440 --> 00:10:06.400
And we filled an entire
building with it I guess.

00:10:07.440 --> 00:10:11.440
So the museum, I mean excuse me,
that exhibit went up in October

00:10:11.440 --> 00:10:12.880
but presumably it came down.

00:10:12.880 --> 00:10:16.480
It was in the corridor
in building 14.

00:10:18.080 --> 00:10:19.120
What happened next?

00:10:19.120 --> 00:10:22.000
Well we started what became
historical collections.

00:10:23.280 --> 00:10:30.000
And with that I
reported directly

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:33.200
to Howard Johnson for the
first two or three years.

00:10:33.200 --> 00:10:34.480
I was the only person
at the institute

00:10:34.480 --> 00:10:35.680
that reported directly to him.

00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:44.960
And that led to a true search
going through every storeroom

00:10:46.480 --> 00:10:47.440
at the Institute.

00:10:48.160 --> 00:10:52.000
And this in turn led to a lot
of interest with the alumni.

00:10:54.080 --> 00:10:59.840
We had all sorts of things that
the alumni were interested in.

00:11:00.480 --> 00:11:03.920
Most MIT alumni would rather
talk about their history

00:11:04.800 --> 00:11:08.080
than they would about the
latest scientific advance.

00:11:08.800 --> 00:11:15.360
And that's true, so we started
drawing a lot of interest

00:11:15.360 --> 00:11:17.520
from alumni.

00:11:18.720 --> 00:11:21.520
And in our fifth
year, which would

00:11:21.520 --> 00:11:25.040
be the fifth year of
existence, probably 76,

00:11:26.640 --> 00:11:32.160
I invited the members of
a class at the reunion

00:11:32.160 --> 00:11:33.920
to come up and see us.

00:11:33.920 --> 00:11:38.240
We were still back in a
small part of the other part

00:11:38.240 --> 00:11:38.720
of the building.

00:11:39.520 --> 00:11:46.720
And as part of that they
were so impressed by what

00:11:47.520 --> 00:11:49.840
they had seen, although
we didn't really

00:11:49.840 --> 00:11:52.560
have an official
exhibits, that they

00:11:53.360 --> 00:12:01.360
both the class of
'25 and '35 suggested

00:12:01.360 --> 00:12:04.320
that MIT collections,
historical collections,

00:12:04.320 --> 00:12:06.320
be given part of
their class gift.

00:12:07.040 --> 00:12:09.840
Well this is a no, no.

00:12:10.880 --> 00:12:16.640
And that got us the
interest of the higher-ups

00:12:16.640 --> 00:12:19.520
and we just had to
fight from there.

00:12:20.880 --> 00:12:24.000
I think John and
Jane particularly,

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:25.760
and Mary of course
is the mastermind

00:12:25.760 --> 00:12:27.200
of keeping track of all this.

00:12:27.200 --> 00:12:29.680
It will come as a bit of
a shock that Warren was

00:12:29.680 --> 00:12:32.240
forbidden from raising money.

00:12:34.320 --> 00:12:37.840
I thought it was compulsory.

00:12:39.880 --> 00:12:40.880
[LAUGHING]

00:12:40.880 --> 00:12:45.440
On that front, Warren,
in the early years

00:12:45.440 --> 00:12:50.720
you had a really intense service
mentality of doing exhibits

00:12:50.720 --> 00:12:54.640
for anyone and everyone who
asked, from the corridors

00:12:54.640 --> 00:12:59.040
at MIT to, I read in
the president's report

00:12:59.040 --> 00:13:03.600
that you even took very early
on a trip to the Florida

00:13:03.600 --> 00:13:06.960
to the Kennedy Space
Center and represented

00:13:07.760 --> 00:13:11.920
MIT and the
historical collections

00:13:11.920 --> 00:13:14.480
to a couple of 100,000
people who visited.

00:13:15.200 --> 00:13:19.760
Tell us a little bit about how
you cultivated that service

00:13:19.760 --> 00:13:23.120
ethos and some of the early team
members who worked with you.

00:13:25.120 --> 00:13:30.080
As far as the audience, well,
if I get the question correctly,

00:13:32.320 --> 00:13:36.000
I started at the request
of the Alumni Association

00:13:36.000 --> 00:13:41.520
to tour [? MIC ?] clubs
around the country.

00:13:41.520 --> 00:13:46.080
And I think I hit every club in
Florida at least three times,

00:13:46.080 --> 00:13:48.320
and there's a lot
of clubs in Florida.

00:13:48.320 --> 00:13:51.760
I hit everywhere from
St. Paul, Minneapolis--

00:13:51.760 --> 00:13:55.120
Minneapolis, St.
Paul, to Montana,

00:13:55.760 --> 00:13:59.680
to actually some in New England.

00:13:59.680 --> 00:14:04.560
So the word spread through
that group that we existed

00:14:04.560 --> 00:14:07.360
and that helped as well.

00:14:07.360 --> 00:14:10.960
So I was invited to
do a lot of things

00:14:10.960 --> 00:14:13.840
that helped spread the word.

00:14:13.840 --> 00:14:17.520
And I should indicate that
there was a tremendous--

00:14:17.520 --> 00:14:18.240
by this time--

00:14:20.800 --> 00:14:22.080
I should say at the
beginning that we

00:14:22.080 --> 00:14:24.240
had no budget, none whatsoever.

00:14:24.960 --> 00:14:29.440
And our main workforce was
Northeastern work study

00:14:29.440 --> 00:14:29.840
students.

00:14:30.480 --> 00:14:33.360
And we owe Northeastern
a tremendous amount

00:14:33.360 --> 00:14:35.120
because we didn't
pay them anything.

00:14:36.000 --> 00:14:39.280
And we continued to have
work study students,

00:14:40.160 --> 00:14:44.240
even after we could finally
start to hire MIT students.

00:14:44.240 --> 00:14:49.360
But then we were able to hire
some staff such as Barbara

00:14:49.360 --> 00:14:55.040
Linden who came from the
archives, was a mainstay.

00:14:55.840 --> 00:15:01.840
We had Michael Yates who had
been work-study student who

00:15:01.840 --> 00:15:03.600
became a regular staff member.

00:15:05.200 --> 00:15:08.880
We built up a very, very
good and talented staff

00:15:09.680 --> 00:15:11.760
of people who were
really dedicated

00:15:11.760 --> 00:15:14.960
and could see a future
in what we were doing.

00:15:16.000 --> 00:15:19.120
So one of your
pivotal acquisitions

00:15:19.120 --> 00:15:22.560
came in '93 when you bought
the holography collection.

00:15:22.560 --> 00:15:26.400
And that's from the
Museum of Holography

00:15:26.400 --> 00:15:29.840
and that's about the time Mary,
you and Jane came on board.

00:15:29.840 --> 00:15:33.200
And I wondered if we might
ask you some questions

00:15:33.200 --> 00:15:35.840
about the mid 1990s.

00:15:35.840 --> 00:15:39.840
Mary, maybe you can reflect
on those first years.

00:15:39.840 --> 00:15:42.960
And really here's
the burning question,

00:15:42.960 --> 00:15:45.760
is it true that MIT wanted
to get rid of the museum

00:15:45.760 --> 00:15:48.640
or thought about getting rid
of the museum in the mid 90s?

00:15:49.600 --> 00:15:52.720
Well I remember Joe
Moses, who was the provost

00:15:52.720 --> 00:15:56.560
to challenge us about what
is the purpose of the museum

00:15:56.560 --> 00:15:59.120
and does it serve
at MIT students.

00:16:00.240 --> 00:16:02.320
So we had to document that.

00:16:02.320 --> 00:16:04.320
I mean, we did then, we do now.

00:16:05.040 --> 00:16:09.280
We're a resource for students
and faculty and classes

00:16:09.280 --> 00:16:12.800
and providing materials,
original materials,

00:16:12.800 --> 00:16:13.680
for their work.

00:16:15.600 --> 00:16:18.720
And I think we also
made the case for how

00:16:18.720 --> 00:16:20.160
the museum serves the public.

00:16:20.160 --> 00:16:23.360
But yes Debbie, we were
challenged by the provost.

00:16:24.720 --> 00:16:26.400
So Jane, you come in.

00:16:27.200 --> 00:16:29.440
What were your first
impressions of this place?

00:16:29.440 --> 00:16:30.400
I mean you come from

00:16:32.320 --> 00:16:32.720
England.

00:16:32.720 --> 00:16:33.644
England.

00:16:33.644 --> 00:16:34.800
[LAUGHING]

00:16:34.800 --> 00:16:35.840
I mean--

00:16:35.840 --> 00:16:38.029
There's a bit of
a conspiracy here.

00:16:38.029 --> 00:16:38.673
[LAUGHING]

00:16:38.673 --> 00:16:39.381
Apparently.

00:16:39.381 --> 00:16:41.120
In case you hadn't noticed.

00:16:41.120 --> 00:16:43.120
So yeah, I was
thinking about that

00:16:43.120 --> 00:16:46.480
because that was a
question that was

00:16:46.480 --> 00:16:48.000
asked when I was interviewed.

00:16:48.000 --> 00:16:51.440
Like why should
MIT have a museum?

00:16:51.440 --> 00:16:54.800
And I remember thinking
that in some ways,

00:16:54.800 --> 00:16:56.880
I mean you always get that
question in a University

00:16:56.880 --> 00:16:57.440
museum, right?

00:16:57.440 --> 00:16:59.920
You're always being asked
about service to the University

00:17:00.560 --> 00:17:03.520
you know like versus service
to the wider community.

00:17:03.520 --> 00:17:05.600
And hopefully the two
actually are the same thing.

00:17:07.360 --> 00:17:10.560
But I remember thinking that
the MIT Museum was almost

00:17:10.560 --> 00:17:14.000
like the purest form
of a University museum

00:17:14.000 --> 00:17:16.480
because it really
was a museum of MIT.

00:17:17.280 --> 00:17:20.240
And so many-- I mean
where I'm working now

00:17:20.240 --> 00:17:22.000
with sort of a museum
of the University

00:17:22.000 --> 00:17:26.680
but not really because we have
a real disciplinary focus.

00:17:26.680 --> 00:17:31.200
Where I'm in an Archaeology
museum right now whereas,

00:17:31.200 --> 00:17:35.520
MIT felt like the museum
could be MIT's museum.

00:17:35.520 --> 00:17:40.560
And I came in and it felt
sort of multidisciplinary

00:17:40.560 --> 00:17:42.400
before that was
sort of fashionable.

00:17:42.400 --> 00:17:45.520
Now everyone does that but
it sort of was before that.

00:17:46.240 --> 00:17:48.560
We had the historical
collections,

00:17:48.560 --> 00:17:50.320
the holography
collections, and I

00:17:50.320 --> 00:17:52.880
remember I'm sure, people here.

00:17:52.880 --> 00:17:58.880
I remember Don Stidsen,
dear person that we miss.

00:17:59.520 --> 00:18:02.320
And Don taking me
around the galleries

00:18:02.320 --> 00:18:04.160
and seeing these
holograms and he was--

00:18:04.880 --> 00:18:07.520
You know how everyone thinks
they know what a hologram is

00:18:07.520 --> 00:18:08.480
and they really don't.

00:18:08.480 --> 00:18:10.400
They think it's something
on a ruler you know?

00:18:11.760 --> 00:18:14.720
And him very patiently
explaining to me

00:18:15.280 --> 00:18:16.880
what actually a hologram was.

00:18:16.880 --> 00:18:18.720
And so then I'm in
the holograms and then

00:18:18.720 --> 00:18:22.560
suddenly I'm in this exhibit
gallery with Arthur Ganson's

00:18:22.560 --> 00:18:23.760
amazing sculptures.

00:18:23.760 --> 00:18:26.960
And it just, it almost
felt liberating that like,

00:18:26.960 --> 00:18:30.560
wow this is a museum that
could really be so many things.

00:18:30.560 --> 00:18:32.720
And so many things
all at the same time.

00:18:33.520 --> 00:18:36.480
But also a sense
of, OK we do need

00:18:36.480 --> 00:18:38.960
some sort of
focused mission that

00:18:38.960 --> 00:18:41.840
thinks about MIT and
particularly the sort of art,

00:18:41.840 --> 00:18:43.200
science issue.

00:18:43.200 --> 00:18:47.440
Just things that the MIT Museum
could be really, really good

00:18:47.440 --> 00:18:51.360
at and so yeah, I think there
was definitely that sort of.

00:18:52.960 --> 00:18:55.600
That's often a pressure, but
yeah there was definitely

00:18:55.600 --> 00:18:56.800
a feeling of that I think.

00:18:57.920 --> 00:19:02.720
John we know that you had
a wife here in Cambridge,

00:19:02.720 --> 00:19:06.240
but you were a thriving
professional in the UK.

00:19:06.240 --> 00:19:10.720
And what is it that
kind of sucked you

00:19:10.720 --> 00:19:15.680
over here, beyond the desire to
maintain good family relations?

00:19:15.680 --> 00:19:18.480
Yeah I was actually spending
too much on air miles

00:19:18.480 --> 00:19:21.680
or whatever it is, in the
period before I came here.

00:19:21.680 --> 00:19:25.280
So there was that,
but I think it's

00:19:25.280 --> 00:19:28.560
rather similar I think to
what Jane's just been saying.

00:19:28.560 --> 00:19:30.640
There's something
about the potential.

00:19:30.640 --> 00:19:33.600
I still think today actually,
the potential of the place

00:19:33.600 --> 00:19:35.280
is far greater than the actual.

00:19:36.080 --> 00:19:39.360
And that's exciting,
that's something to go for.

00:19:39.360 --> 00:19:42.240
I mean, it's not very
inspiring to be in a place

00:19:42.240 --> 00:19:43.920
where you think,
well it's pretty much

00:19:43.920 --> 00:19:47.920
done everything it can and
it's just a matter of cruising.

00:19:47.920 --> 00:19:49.440
Who wants to be in such a place?

00:19:49.440 --> 00:19:50.640
I don't know, but not me.

00:19:50.640 --> 00:19:54.240
So I was very attracted to that.

00:19:55.200 --> 00:19:57.520
It is a feature of
museums in general

00:19:57.520 --> 00:20:02.480
I think that they are
in a certain sense

00:20:02.480 --> 00:20:05.680
ambiguous as to what
their true character is.

00:20:05.680 --> 00:20:08.960
I mean, I'll make an analogy
with academic departments.

00:20:08.960 --> 00:20:11.680
If you ask, well, what
exactly is the Department

00:20:11.680 --> 00:20:13.360
of Aeronautics and Astronautics?

00:20:13.360 --> 00:20:15.760
I mean you don't need to ask
because it's kind of obvious.

00:20:16.560 --> 00:20:21.360
But if you ask me what
exactly is the Gardner Museum?

00:20:21.360 --> 00:20:24.320
Or what exactly
is the MIT Museum?

00:20:24.320 --> 00:20:26.640
It seems as if the question
needs to be answered.

00:20:26.640 --> 00:20:29.520
And it's not-- I'm not
original in pointing out

00:20:29.520 --> 00:20:32.160
that it's a great
advantage of museums

00:20:32.160 --> 00:20:34.480
that they have this ambiguity.

00:20:34.480 --> 00:20:37.280
Because they can be molded
and shaped and they can

00:20:37.280 --> 00:20:38.480
do all sorts of things.

00:20:38.480 --> 00:20:43.040
And I think exploring the
envelope of what you could do

00:20:43.760 --> 00:20:45.440
and what might be
interesting and what

00:20:45.440 --> 00:20:48.080
might be productive is
part of what makes it a joy

00:20:48.080 --> 00:20:49.680
to work in a place like this.

00:20:49.680 --> 00:20:51.440
It feels very MIT.

00:20:51.440 --> 00:20:51.680
Yes

00:20:52.480 --> 00:20:56.960
I've worked in many different
universities so it feels--

00:20:56.960 --> 00:20:59.280
sometimes I'm in a
situation where I think,

00:20:59.840 --> 00:21:02.160
oh yeah I remember
that happened at Yale.

00:21:02.160 --> 00:21:02.640
You know?

00:21:02.640 --> 00:21:05.040
And you're trying not
to sound all super

00:21:05.040 --> 00:21:07.040
like, oh I worked in all
these great universities,

00:21:07.040 --> 00:21:10.000
but MIT is different.

00:21:10.000 --> 00:21:12.240
I mean I can safely
say that having worked

00:21:12.240 --> 00:21:16.880
in several universities at this
point, but it felt like a place

00:21:16.880 --> 00:21:18.800
where you could do all
that and actually it

00:21:18.800 --> 00:21:21.520
was encouraged, and
was sort of typical

00:21:21.520 --> 00:21:24.160
of the rest of the community.

00:21:24.160 --> 00:21:25.920
One thing I will
just comment on, I'm

00:21:25.920 --> 00:21:27.920
interested to know
if this was true

00:21:27.920 --> 00:21:32.080
also for all of my other
colleagues on the bench

00:21:32.080 --> 00:21:34.640
here or whatever on, the panel.

00:21:36.080 --> 00:21:37.920
MIT likes to know
what's going on

00:21:37.920 --> 00:21:40.080
and there is a whole
administrative structure.

00:21:40.080 --> 00:21:44.800
No one has ever
questioned me about why

00:21:44.800 --> 00:21:48.480
we're exhibiting this, or
why we chose that topic.

00:21:48.480 --> 00:21:49.680
Why are you doing
this thing called

00:21:49.680 --> 00:21:54.000
the Enemy, which was all
about international conflict,

00:21:55.280 --> 00:21:58.000
or intra-national conflict
actually, as well.

00:21:58.000 --> 00:22:00.800
And there was a great liberty
and there is a great liberty

00:22:00.800 --> 00:22:02.720
to explore, to experiment.

00:22:03.360 --> 00:22:04.560
Was that always true?

00:22:04.560 --> 00:22:06.640
I mean, Warren was
that true for you?

00:22:06.640 --> 00:22:09.680
Did you feel under pressure
to exhibit certain things

00:22:09.680 --> 00:22:12.640
or were people happy for
you to do more or less

00:22:12.640 --> 00:22:14.560
whatever you and your
colleagues decided?

00:22:16.640 --> 00:22:18.400
If I understand, we just--

00:22:19.840 --> 00:22:22.480
we could do anything we
wanted as far as exhibits

00:22:23.200 --> 00:22:25.320
because we had no guideline.

00:22:25.840 --> 00:22:28.560
We had wonderful collections and
they were building collections

00:22:29.120 --> 00:22:31.120
so we took it.

00:22:31.120 --> 00:22:34.240
After we developed the
exhibit space in here

00:22:34.240 --> 00:22:38.240
we also took over Compton
gallery in the main campus.

00:22:38.240 --> 00:22:41.360
So we had a really good
exhibit space there

00:22:41.360 --> 00:22:43.600
and then we took over
the Hart Nautical

00:22:44.480 --> 00:22:46.400
Collections and gallery.

00:22:46.400 --> 00:22:50.560
And so we just had
as wide of variety

00:22:50.560 --> 00:22:53.840
as possible from
Charles Woodbury,

00:22:54.880 --> 00:22:57.200
who is an American artist who--

00:22:58.240 --> 00:23:00.000
early American
impressionist which

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:05.120
has happened in [? Stewart. ?]
He graduated in 1888,

00:23:05.120 --> 00:23:05.520
I believe.

00:23:06.400 --> 00:23:09.280
And so we could do
whatever we wanted to.

00:23:09.280 --> 00:23:11.280
No one told us anything.

00:23:12.000 --> 00:23:14.240
And we did have an
advisory committee which

00:23:14.240 --> 00:23:16.800
was very supportive
in those early days

00:23:16.800 --> 00:23:20.800
and helped us open doors and
find materials and so forth.

00:23:20.800 --> 00:23:23.600
Warren, you raised a question.

00:23:23.600 --> 00:23:26.800
You shared, Woodbury I
know is one of your loves

00:23:26.800 --> 00:23:31.440
but I'm curious with the others
if you have particular objects

00:23:31.440 --> 00:23:34.800
or collections or
programs or projects

00:23:34.800 --> 00:23:37.040
that you've had a
particular passion

00:23:37.040 --> 00:23:40.160
for during your tenure here.

00:23:42.000 --> 00:23:43.040
Well I'll share.

00:23:43.760 --> 00:23:46.240
I'm very interested in
architecture and design

00:23:46.240 --> 00:23:48.880
so I love the
architecture and design

00:23:48.880 --> 00:23:51.840
collection which comes out
of the [? Bosar ?] tradition.

00:23:52.720 --> 00:23:56.080
We have incredible
drawings and plans

00:23:56.720 --> 00:24:02.080
and we had the opportunity when
the architectural collaborative

00:24:02.720 --> 00:24:07.040
firm was disbanding to
acquire some of their archive.

00:24:07.040 --> 00:24:09.840
And I'm afraid that I was
helping with the dumpster

00:24:09.840 --> 00:24:12.960
diving and rescuing
some of those plans.

00:24:13.760 --> 00:24:16.480
But they, as it turns
out, they are now

00:24:16.480 --> 00:24:19.840
one of, if I'm not mistaken,
one of the most requested

00:24:19.840 --> 00:24:23.520
collections because there
are many buildings designed

00:24:23.520 --> 00:24:28.400
by the firm TAC, as it's
known, that are being restored

00:24:28.400 --> 00:24:30.880
or are being worked on.

00:24:30.880 --> 00:24:36.080
And so the documentary
evidence is really important.

00:24:36.960 --> 00:24:40.720
So that's one of my
favorite collections.

00:24:40.720 --> 00:24:44.400
So that's totally brilliant
Mary because I sort of

00:24:44.400 --> 00:24:45.840
liked all the others.

00:24:47.000 --> 00:24:48.000
[LAUGHING]

00:24:48.000 --> 00:24:50.320
Not that I have anything
against architecture.

00:24:51.520 --> 00:24:56.560
But I sort of was like,
oh wow the history,

00:24:57.760 --> 00:24:59.840
the hacks, the holograms.

00:24:59.840 --> 00:25:01.120
There's a lot of H's in that.

00:25:02.320 --> 00:25:03.360
And then the heart.

00:25:03.360 --> 00:25:05.040
Oh my God that's four H's.

00:25:05.040 --> 00:25:06.960
OK and the Hart
Nautical Collections.

00:25:06.960 --> 00:25:11.120
So for me it was just
so exciting to see

00:25:11.120 --> 00:25:14.560
all these different things,
many of which I knew zero about.

00:25:14.560 --> 00:25:16.480
I remember coming in
and saying to people,

00:25:17.200 --> 00:25:20.880
I'm like, she says
modestly, the best director

00:25:20.880 --> 00:25:23.040
to have because I know
absolutely nothing about any

00:25:23.040 --> 00:25:23.680
of these things.

00:25:23.680 --> 00:25:26.720
So I can let you get on all
these amazing curatorial

00:25:26.720 --> 00:25:27.920
and collection stuff.

00:25:27.920 --> 00:25:32.480
You guys can get on and tell me
what you need and let's think

00:25:32.480 --> 00:25:33.760
of a vision and let's--

00:25:34.320 --> 00:25:38.000
but I'm not-- I don't know one
end of a yacht from another.

00:25:39.120 --> 00:25:43.040
I learned quite a lot about
the history of science and MIT

00:25:43.040 --> 00:25:45.360
culture pretty quickly.

00:25:47.200 --> 00:25:50.960
But yeah, so it's
hard for me to pick.

00:25:50.960 --> 00:25:52.640
For each of us it's different.

00:25:52.640 --> 00:25:55.200
Yeah so it's not that I want to
say I don't like architecture,

00:25:55.200 --> 00:25:56.640
Gary, I wouldn't say that.

00:25:56.640 --> 00:25:59.200
But since Mary said that
I thought I could say it.

00:25:59.200 --> 00:26:00.800
I have a different
sort of answer

00:26:00.800 --> 00:26:02.720
because I love
lots of the stuff.

00:26:02.720 --> 00:26:05.040
But the thing I
really remember being

00:26:05.040 --> 00:26:06.880
so pleased about
when I first came

00:26:07.600 --> 00:26:09.840
was that this museum
actively collects.

00:26:11.360 --> 00:26:13.360
Because I had a
previous experience

00:26:13.360 --> 00:26:16.800
in a very large prestigious
museum, which I will not name,

00:26:17.440 --> 00:26:18.960
since I'm now going
to criticize it.

00:26:19.520 --> 00:26:21.520
Which had such a
large collection

00:26:21.520 --> 00:26:22.960
and such a sense
of self-importance

00:26:22.960 --> 00:26:24.880
that it had largely
stopped collecting.

00:26:25.920 --> 00:26:30.000
And I struggled there to
try and persuade people

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:33.520
that science and technology,
which was what this place did,

00:26:33.520 --> 00:26:37.600
hadn't stopped and they ought
to be collecting modern stuff.

00:26:37.600 --> 00:26:40.800
And when I came here, as I
think we probably all have done,

00:26:41.520 --> 00:26:43.600
I'm guessing with
Warren I'm afraid.

00:26:43.600 --> 00:26:46.800
There was a collections
committee and I duly attended.

00:26:46.800 --> 00:26:50.080
And every single meeting
their new acquisitions.

00:26:50.720 --> 00:26:54.080
And Gary and Debbie and
the rest of the curators

00:26:54.080 --> 00:26:56.480
are coming forward and
saying look at this thing.

00:26:56.480 --> 00:26:58.880
And we just collect--

00:26:58.880 --> 00:27:01.600
sometimes we collect things and
you think, well I suppose so.

00:27:02.240 --> 00:27:05.840
But more often it's like, wow!

00:27:06.400 --> 00:27:09.760
I remember the day, it was
probably more than 10 years ago

00:27:09.760 --> 00:27:10.720
now, Debbie will know.

00:27:11.760 --> 00:27:16.560
When we had the opportunity to
collect a whole series of 1950s

00:27:16.560 --> 00:27:20.800
and '60s toys,
children's toys that

00:27:20.800 --> 00:27:26.400
were made by Claude Shannon, one
of the founders of information

00:27:26.400 --> 00:27:27.440
science and technology.

00:27:27.440 --> 00:27:29.200
And he made these
things to amuse kids

00:27:29.200 --> 00:27:31.040
because he thought it
was boring, I think,

00:27:31.040 --> 00:27:36.800
to go buy a store toy when you
could design something better.

00:27:36.800 --> 00:27:37.600
And they were better.

00:27:37.600 --> 00:27:38.560
I mean they're amazing.

00:27:38.560 --> 00:27:42.800
Juggling machines and one of
the world's first chess playing

00:27:42.800 --> 00:27:46.080
machines, we have these things
in the collection it's amazing.

00:27:47.280 --> 00:27:49.440
Some of them will even
go into the new museum.

00:27:49.440 --> 00:27:52.880
That's the thrill for me of
being here that the collections

00:27:52.880 --> 00:27:53.600
keep doing this.

00:27:54.240 --> 00:27:57.589
Which we have to have a
talk with associate provost

00:27:57.589 --> 00:27:58.800
Phillip [? Coorey ?]
and the provost

00:27:58.800 --> 00:28:01.680
occasionally about
because it means that we

00:28:01.680 --> 00:28:02.800
keep running out of space.

00:28:02.800 --> 00:28:04.320
But that's another story.

00:28:04.320 --> 00:28:06.880
Well, and the great thing
about the collections committee

00:28:06.880 --> 00:28:09.040
and I'm sure this
was Warren's doing,

00:28:09.040 --> 00:28:11.360
was having Claude
Brenner as the chair

00:28:11.360 --> 00:28:13.120
of that while I was there.

00:28:13.120 --> 00:28:17.840
And he was someone that I
dearly loved and was so kind

00:28:17.840 --> 00:28:21.440
to me, especially at the
beginning when I was like, I

00:28:21.440 --> 00:28:22.800
don't know anything about MIT.

00:28:22.800 --> 00:28:27.040
And he sort of would talk with
me and he had so many stories.

00:28:27.040 --> 00:28:29.920
And I'm assuming that
you knew him well.

00:28:29.920 --> 00:28:30.640
Warren.

00:28:30.640 --> 00:28:32.320
And yeah that was--

00:28:32.960 --> 00:28:34.960
he was a great person.

00:28:34.960 --> 00:28:37.680
And he ran the collections
committee really, really well.

00:28:37.680 --> 00:28:37.920
[LAUGHING]

00:28:40.080 --> 00:28:43.680
I am curious, you've shared
about your love of collections,

00:28:43.680 --> 00:28:46.960
but I have a lot of exhibitions
and programs colleagues here.

00:28:46.960 --> 00:28:50.080
I'm wondering if you
might give some--

00:28:52.080 --> 00:28:57.280
share some of your most
enjoyable innovations.

00:28:57.280 --> 00:29:00.240
I'm thinking of [? FAD ?] and
[? FAST ?] and [? Soap Box, ?]

00:29:00.240 --> 00:29:03.360
and of course, we can't--
the Science Festival.

00:29:03.360 --> 00:29:09.040
And I'm just wondering if
you might share some stories

00:29:09.040 --> 00:29:13.200
about some of your favorite,
or memorable, or challenging

00:29:14.400 --> 00:29:20.160
programs and/or exhibitions
that you undertook

00:29:21.120 --> 00:29:22.320
during your tenure here.

00:29:23.920 --> 00:29:31.200
Well to me, it's amazing
how quickly we got support.

00:29:31.200 --> 00:29:33.280
But I would point
out one example,

00:29:35.760 --> 00:29:40.320
Al Hill was a vice president
of research, I think.

00:29:41.280 --> 00:29:43.120
And he asked us--

00:29:43.120 --> 00:29:46.400
they were coming to me,
he had been coming to MIT

00:29:46.400 --> 00:29:47.760
as part of the Rad Lab.

00:29:48.560 --> 00:29:52.320
And he wanted to do something to
celebrate while there are still

00:29:52.320 --> 00:29:53.920
a lot of people
around to celebrate

00:29:53.920 --> 00:29:57.360
the history of the Rad Lab.

00:29:57.360 --> 00:29:59.600
So he asked us if
we would go down

00:29:59.600 --> 00:30:02.880
to outside of
Harrisburg, Pennsylvania

00:30:03.520 --> 00:30:07.440
to choose a few photographs
from the collection which

00:30:07.440 --> 00:30:12.240
had been taken directly from
MIT and put into federal storage

00:30:12.240 --> 00:30:12.480
there.

00:30:13.040 --> 00:30:18.480
So we get down there
and found very quickly

00:30:19.360 --> 00:30:24.000
that it was a huge
collection, that it was also

00:30:24.000 --> 00:30:25.920
due to be just destroyed.

00:30:25.920 --> 00:30:29.920
It was in the channel to be
destroyed within the next six

00:30:29.920 --> 00:30:30.240
months.

00:30:31.040 --> 00:30:34.560
So I call Al Hill and
he called right back

00:30:34.560 --> 00:30:38.640
and said, rent a truck and
bring the whole thing back.

00:30:38.640 --> 00:30:42.320
So we got the whole history
of, photographic history,

00:30:42.320 --> 00:30:45.600
of the Rad Lab just
in one fell swoop.

00:30:45.600 --> 00:30:50.160
And that to me, is an
exceedingly important part

00:30:50.160 --> 00:30:51.280
of MIT's story.

00:30:51.280 --> 00:30:55.680
The Rad Lab and how
it transformed MIT

00:30:55.680 --> 00:31:00.560
into a top flight-- not that
it wasn't top flight before,

00:31:00.560 --> 00:31:03.840
but a top flight research
University as well as

00:31:04.400 --> 00:31:05.120
other things.

00:31:05.120 --> 00:31:08.240
But I could go through so
many of the collections that

00:31:08.240 --> 00:31:10.560
came in the same way,
came in as a bulk.

00:31:11.120 --> 00:31:14.000
Like the hologram
collection for example,

00:31:14.000 --> 00:31:17.280
we bought-- we were
sent to an auction

00:31:17.280 --> 00:31:19.360
to pick up a few
pieces that Steve

00:31:19.360 --> 00:31:22.000
wanted to have here at MIT.

00:31:22.960 --> 00:31:28.400
And we ended up getting the
entire collection of holograms

00:31:29.360 --> 00:31:31.840
because they wanted to hold
the collection together.

00:31:31.840 --> 00:31:34.160
How man holograms
was that, Warren?

00:31:34.160 --> 00:31:36.000
How many holograms was that?

00:31:36.000 --> 00:31:37.200
Oh I don't know.

00:31:37.200 --> 00:31:37.760
We had--

00:31:37.760 --> 00:31:39.120
More than two thousand.

00:31:39.120 --> 00:31:40.480
More than two thousand.

00:31:40.480 --> 00:31:41.840
Largest collection in the world.

00:31:42.560 --> 00:31:44.080
It's by far the best--

00:31:44.080 --> 00:31:44.800
Just like that.

00:31:44.800 --> 00:31:46.080
We just happened to
get the whole thing.

00:31:46.080 --> 00:31:50.080
It's like-- Warren
mentioned the reunions.

00:31:50.080 --> 00:31:52.240
And I have to say
that the reunion

00:31:52.240 --> 00:31:57.280
event around like the Rad Lab
the whirlwind computer, people,

00:31:57.280 --> 00:31:59.680
we did it with
lasers, we've done it

00:31:59.680 --> 00:32:02.960
with Lincoln Laboratory,
a number of occasions.

00:32:03.840 --> 00:32:08.160
The museum has hosted
really significant events

00:32:08.160 --> 00:32:13.520
with pioneers in computing
and radar and the like.

00:32:13.520 --> 00:32:16.080
And I'd have to say
Warren, you really

00:32:16.080 --> 00:32:18.560
innovated the
reunion event which

00:32:18.560 --> 00:32:22.160
often had an accompanying
exhibition, program,

00:32:23.440 --> 00:32:24.720
gatherings with people.

00:32:24.720 --> 00:32:29.600
It's one of the first most
successful types of programs

00:32:29.600 --> 00:32:30.960
this museum had.

00:32:31.840 --> 00:32:34.960
They had opportunity for
collecting oral histories,

00:32:34.960 --> 00:32:38.000
which are so important
for documenting

00:32:39.120 --> 00:32:40.480
whatever the activity is.

00:32:41.040 --> 00:32:44.880
But I know Jane, you and
John really also began

00:32:44.880 --> 00:32:49.600
to push outward and bring
some new kinds of programs

00:32:49.600 --> 00:32:52.480
for families and
other audiences.

00:32:52.480 --> 00:32:56.560
And I wonder if you might
tell some stories about those.

00:32:56.560 --> 00:32:58.400
So I was thinking
about that because you

00:32:58.400 --> 00:33:00.000
had mentioned that last week.

00:33:00.000 --> 00:33:02.160
And of course, the
Friday after Thanksgiving

00:33:02.160 --> 00:33:04.240
science was brilliant,
I have to say.

00:33:04.240 --> 00:33:07.600
Nothing to do with me, but just
really, really great stuff.

00:33:07.600 --> 00:33:10.480
That come up with an idea and
you're like, that's the idea.

00:33:11.520 --> 00:33:13.840
And now I can't remember
what FAST stands for other

00:33:13.840 --> 00:33:15.280
than science and technology.

00:33:15.280 --> 00:33:17.680
Family Adventures in
Science and Technology.

00:33:17.680 --> 00:33:19.680
Yes and I remember
we started that.

00:33:20.560 --> 00:33:24.000
And that was really great but
you know thanks to Arthur.

00:33:24.000 --> 00:33:28.000
And then the other thing I
really enjoyed and I remember

00:33:28.000 --> 00:33:30.400
because it was just as
you were starting Debbie,

00:33:31.760 --> 00:33:34.960
was the exhibition on
artificial intelligence.

00:33:34.960 --> 00:33:37.680
It was called-- was it called
robots and beyond then?

00:33:37.680 --> 00:33:37.920
Yeah

00:33:37.920 --> 00:33:41.360
Yeah, so anyway we
did that because I

00:33:41.360 --> 00:33:47.360
remember we were slightly short
staffed, as often was the case.

00:33:48.080 --> 00:33:50.880
And so I ended up-- and
this was pretty Debbie,

00:33:51.920 --> 00:33:54.720
I ended up doing a lot
about the history of AI,

00:33:54.720 --> 00:33:56.320
which I knew nothing about.

00:33:56.880 --> 00:33:59.840
But working with Rob
Brooks and other members

00:33:59.840 --> 00:34:03.760
of his lab and everything,
which was really fun.

00:34:04.720 --> 00:34:08.000
And then spending ages
getting this actually

00:34:08.000 --> 00:34:12.800
non-MIT electronic robot
from Bristol, England, which

00:34:12.800 --> 00:34:15.440
was on loan to the Smithsonian.

00:34:15.440 --> 00:34:18.000
Oh no, it was at the
Smithsonian and we

00:34:18.000 --> 00:34:21.360
got this loan and Rob
Brooks just being,

00:34:21.360 --> 00:34:23.360
he said to me there's
this thing and I've always

00:34:23.360 --> 00:34:24.320
wanted to have it.

00:34:24.320 --> 00:34:25.120
Was it the Turpil?

00:34:26.640 --> 00:34:30.400
Yes, the [? turtle. ?]
Yeah, so managing and being

00:34:30.400 --> 00:34:33.280
so impressed by the
power of the internet

00:34:33.280 --> 00:34:36.880
because I was able to track
this down and at the time it

00:34:36.880 --> 00:34:38.160
wasn't quite like today.

00:34:38.160 --> 00:34:41.520
But anyway, so I loved
that, doing that exhibit.

00:34:42.640 --> 00:34:45.520
And it felt like because the
other exhibits had sort of come

00:34:45.520 --> 00:34:50.000
in under your tenure and Mary's
with the holograms and Arthur

00:34:50.000 --> 00:34:51.920
Ganson sculptures,
but that was something

00:34:51.920 --> 00:34:55.360
that we did that
was sort of new.

00:34:56.240 --> 00:34:58.692
And then [INAUDIBLE] of
course felt very special.

00:34:58.692 --> 00:35:01.680
And just as a credit to
what you're saying Jane,

00:35:01.680 --> 00:35:06.880
I mean we didn't finally
manage to stop the Friday

00:35:06.880 --> 00:35:09.120
after Thanksgiving chain
reaction until just

00:35:09.120 --> 00:35:10.080
a couple of years ago.

00:35:10.080 --> 00:35:13.120
I mean, the reason was
that Arthur Ganson

00:35:13.680 --> 00:35:15.600
whose idea it probably had been?

00:35:15.600 --> 00:35:17.440
It was him and one of our staff.

00:35:17.440 --> 00:35:19.200
Was it Marcia?

00:35:19.200 --> 00:35:20.240
And Kathy [? Kirsten. ?]

00:35:20.240 --> 00:35:24.080
So Marcia and Cassie and
Arthur sort of collectively

00:35:24.080 --> 00:35:24.720
came up with it.

00:35:24.720 --> 00:35:26.400
Well, by the time
I got here Arthur

00:35:26.400 --> 00:35:28.960
was the impresario
who was overseeing

00:35:28.960 --> 00:35:30.720
this extraordinary
chain reaction.

00:35:31.280 --> 00:35:34.800
And he also made the
final link in the chain.

00:35:34.800 --> 00:35:38.000
So he was kind of essential
and each year we'd

00:35:38.000 --> 00:35:40.000
say, well would you
mind doing it next year?

00:35:40.000 --> 00:35:41.120
Oh all right then.

00:35:41.120 --> 00:35:43.680
And then, I might make
next year my last year.

00:35:43.680 --> 00:35:45.680
And then you get,
well perhaps one more.

00:35:46.560 --> 00:35:50.480
And this went on and on
and on and it went on

00:35:50.480 --> 00:35:52.480
because it, you're
absolutely right,

00:35:52.480 --> 00:35:55.680
it was just right
for the MIT Museum.

00:35:55.680 --> 00:35:58.720
And we would have, in the
latter stages of the program,

00:35:58.720 --> 00:36:02.800
we'd have the best part of
1,000 people on bleacher

00:36:02.800 --> 00:36:08.560
seats looking at all these
teams making extended chain

00:36:08.560 --> 00:36:10.000
reactions in a gymnasium.

00:36:10.000 --> 00:36:13.360
I mean just-- and I
hope that tradition

00:36:13.360 --> 00:36:17.040
will carry on in new ways, I
must say, in the new museum.

00:36:17.040 --> 00:36:19.120
My choice of
exhibition, it's very

00:36:19.120 --> 00:36:21.200
difficult but since you
asked, I mean of course

00:36:21.200 --> 00:36:24.560
I have a special fondness for
the Cambridge Science Festival.

00:36:24.560 --> 00:36:27.760
Not least because I always
suspect that putting up

00:36:27.760 --> 00:36:32.720
the genome trail, which
must rank as one of the most

00:36:32.720 --> 00:36:34.960
difficult exhibits
I ever oversaw,

00:36:35.760 --> 00:36:40.720
along the street between
Kendall and Harvard Square

00:36:40.720 --> 00:36:42.000
for the first year
of the festival.

00:36:42.000 --> 00:36:44.880
I think it went right
outside Phil Sharp's office.

00:36:44.880 --> 00:36:47.200
And he saw this curious
structure on the street

00:36:47.200 --> 00:36:49.360
and that's how we came to
get the link with Phil which

00:36:49.920 --> 00:36:51.440
has been very important to us.

00:36:51.440 --> 00:36:54.800
So I have a double reason
for liking the festival.

00:36:54.800 --> 00:36:56.640
But when it comes
to exhibitions I

00:36:56.640 --> 00:36:58.880
think I've just got a
shout for this thing

00:36:58.880 --> 00:37:00.560
we did recently
called the Enemy.

00:37:01.200 --> 00:37:04.640
Which happened in this space
and it was an entirely virtual

00:37:04.640 --> 00:37:05.760
reality exhibition.

00:37:06.320 --> 00:37:09.120
It was a great achievement
by our exhibitions team

00:37:09.120 --> 00:37:09.760
to pull it off.

00:37:09.760 --> 00:37:13.200
It was also a collaboration with
the Center for Art, Science,

00:37:13.200 --> 00:37:15.040
and Technology [INAUDIBLE] here.

00:37:15.680 --> 00:37:19.600
It was very, very hard to
do and very, very memorable

00:37:19.600 --> 00:37:21.920
for anybody who actually went
into the exhibition was like,

00:37:21.920 --> 00:37:23.680
you've never done
anything like this before.

00:37:24.560 --> 00:37:26.640
And to me it was
a special thing.&nbsp;

00:37:28.240 --> 00:37:30.960
I did want to point
out one factor that

00:37:31.840 --> 00:37:34.640
played a critical role in
building the collections

00:37:34.640 --> 00:37:36.160
and getting word out.

00:37:37.520 --> 00:37:40.800
We had our first two
students that we,

00:37:40.800 --> 00:37:42.960
MIT students that we
hired in the museum.

00:37:43.680 --> 00:37:45.680
We had no money to
pay them of course,

00:37:45.680 --> 00:37:47.120
but that didn't stop us.

00:37:48.560 --> 00:37:55.600
So we-- One was Scott
Ferguson, class of '78 and he

00:37:56.720 --> 00:38:02.240
organized a trip, a
tour of every store room

00:38:02.240 --> 00:38:05.280
on campus, literally
every store room.

00:38:05.280 --> 00:38:07.280
And we worked with
the department

00:38:08.080 --> 00:38:11.760
administrative officer
who I knew from my time

00:38:11.760 --> 00:38:17.840
in the humanities and we
found phenomenal stuff.

00:38:18.400 --> 00:38:22.160
It's just-- we filled the
basement of it here with that.

00:38:22.960 --> 00:38:26.800
The other one of those first
to students was David--

00:38:30.320 --> 00:38:31.840
I forgot.

00:38:32.720 --> 00:38:33.280
David Carp?

00:38:33.920 --> 00:38:35.200
David Carp, yes of course.

00:38:36.400 --> 00:38:45.760
David had a special knack
for working with film.

00:38:45.760 --> 00:38:50.880
And we had taken over a
staggering amount of MIT film

00:38:50.880 --> 00:38:53.280
that had come from various
departments and so forth.

00:38:53.920 --> 00:38:59.200
And one of the things he did
was to do a film, just as

00:38:59.200 --> 00:39:03.840
an example, he did a film
we had in the collection

00:39:03.840 --> 00:39:06.880
but had never been
restored or shown.

00:39:07.600 --> 00:39:13.280
A film taken in the early
'30s at the at the engineering

00:39:13.280 --> 00:39:19.040
camp in Down East
Maine [? Mathias. ?]

00:39:19.040 --> 00:39:25.680
And that film was
shown to the class

00:39:25.680 --> 00:39:34.800
of '35 and one of the leaders
of the class was watching it

00:39:34.800 --> 00:39:38.000
and big tears were rolling down
his eyes because he couldn't

00:39:38.000 --> 00:39:44.000
believe that the film from 1931
of him being in East Mathias

00:39:44.000 --> 00:39:44.960
even existed.

00:39:45.680 --> 00:39:49.440
And this I mean, that shows
you how important and--

00:39:50.640 --> 00:39:53.520
that's, I guess that's
why I'm an honorary member

00:39:53.520 --> 00:39:54.640
of the class of '35.

00:39:54.640 --> 00:39:57.040
But that's another
matter entirely.

00:39:57.760 --> 00:40:00.160
I have to tell a
secret story though

00:40:00.160 --> 00:40:02.160
that Scott shared with
us a couple of years

00:40:02.160 --> 00:40:03.360
ago when he visited.

00:40:03.360 --> 00:40:08.080
On this tour, this was when
MIT, everything was keyed

00:40:08.080 --> 00:40:11.040
and it's the pre Schlage
system, all Yale locks.

00:40:11.040 --> 00:40:14.000
And there was actually
a campus master key.

00:40:15.840 --> 00:40:19.920
And Warren, you had
made arrangements

00:40:20.960 --> 00:40:24.240
to get hold of one of
these keys to facilitate

00:40:24.240 --> 00:40:27.840
this inspection of all these
closets all over the place.

00:40:27.840 --> 00:40:29.440
So that they didn't
have to pester

00:40:29.440 --> 00:40:33.040
department chairmen and the
like to get into these places.

00:40:34.720 --> 00:40:38.400
Scott revealed to me later is
Warren, you gave them this key

00:40:38.400 --> 00:40:42.160
and you made them swear six
ways to Sunday that they weren't

00:40:42.160 --> 00:40:43.280
going to lose it,
they weren't going

00:40:43.280 --> 00:40:46.720
to give it to anyone, that
this was very precious, how

00:40:46.720 --> 00:40:47.600
special this was.

00:40:48.160 --> 00:40:50.960
And Scott says we
just couldn't tell you

00:40:50.960 --> 00:40:53.680
Warren, that we already had
a key because we were part

00:40:53.680 --> 00:40:55.440
of the hacking group on campus.

00:41:00.560 --> 00:41:04.000
So I'm curious about some of
your challenges that you faced.

00:41:04.000 --> 00:41:05.680
Are there some things
that you thought

00:41:05.680 --> 00:41:09.600
were going to be super easy
to do and then just turn out

00:41:09.600 --> 00:41:12.000
to be nightmarishly difficult?

00:41:12.000 --> 00:41:16.240
Or are there some
things that you thought

00:41:16.240 --> 00:41:22.720
would be really hard that just
seemed to-- all the pieces

00:41:22.720 --> 00:41:23.920
seem to fall into place?

00:41:24.800 --> 00:41:28.400
Arthur Ganson was invited to
create a sculpture for

00:41:29.440 --> 00:41:30.960
one of these windows here.

00:41:30.960 --> 00:41:35.760
And he created a version of,
it's called Margot's Other Cat.

00:41:35.760 --> 00:41:38.240
And most of his sculptures,
if you know them,

00:41:38.240 --> 00:41:40.640
are pretty intimate,
small scale.

00:41:41.200 --> 00:41:46.480
This was an experiment for
him where it was exploded.

00:41:46.480 --> 00:41:50.800
And he had all these
gears and this stuff.

00:41:50.800 --> 00:41:53.680
I mean it was a stuffed
cat, it was a toy cat

00:41:54.320 --> 00:41:56.080
and it was being flung around.

00:41:56.080 --> 00:42:00.560
And the animal rights people
in this People's Republic

00:42:00.560 --> 00:42:01.840
of Cambridge--

00:42:01.840 --> 00:42:02.800
[LAUGHING]

00:42:02.800 --> 00:42:06.480
People's Republic of Cambridge
came forward and were outraged.

00:42:06.480 --> 00:42:08.320
I forgot about that.

00:42:08.320 --> 00:42:09.040
Outraged.

00:42:09.040 --> 00:42:12.720
And so what we thought would
be a very effective, at street

00:42:12.720 --> 00:42:17.280
level, attractor to the
museum had to be removed.

00:42:18.480 --> 00:42:19.280
It was censored.

00:42:21.040 --> 00:42:22.160
Do you remember that Jane?

00:42:22.160 --> 00:42:23.120
I didn't.

00:42:23.120 --> 00:42:26.240
I remember doing-- we
did the whole thing,

00:42:26.240 --> 00:42:29.120
but I didn't remember
that, Oh my God.

00:42:29.120 --> 00:42:30.320
That's amazing.

00:42:30.320 --> 00:42:32.720
Actually it's hard for
me to remember challenges

00:42:32.720 --> 00:42:35.360
because Mary dealt with
so much that I'm not

00:42:35.360 --> 00:42:39.440
sure I was actually aware of all
the things that were going on.

00:42:41.840 --> 00:42:43.360
My biggest challenge
when I came was

00:42:43.360 --> 00:42:46.320
that I came with a fixed
idea, as Debbie knows well,

00:42:46.880 --> 00:42:49.120
and some other colleagues
who will know too,

00:42:49.120 --> 00:42:51.840
I was going to move this
museum into the Metropolitan

00:42:51.840 --> 00:42:52.400
warehouse.

00:42:53.120 --> 00:42:54.800
And it was going
to take five years.

00:42:54.800 --> 00:42:57.600
But if you look at the first
strategic plan that we oversaw,

00:42:58.720 --> 00:43:02.000
in my time, that was
the lead message,

00:43:02.000 --> 00:43:04.560
by the end of this plan will
be in the metro warehouse.

00:43:04.560 --> 00:43:07.760
If that had been done, we'd
have been in the metro warehouse

00:43:07.760 --> 00:43:08.880
in 2010.

00:43:09.520 --> 00:43:12.880
And it probably is obvious to
you all that we didn't do that.

00:43:12.880 --> 00:43:14.880
And the reason was
that was much harder

00:43:14.880 --> 00:43:16.400
to pull off than I realized.

00:43:16.400 --> 00:43:17.760
And it was impossible, in fact.

00:43:19.120 --> 00:43:22.160
I do remember seeing that
strategic plan and thinking,

00:43:22.160 --> 00:43:26.160
hmm.

00:43:26.160 --> 00:43:26.240
[LAUGHING]

00:43:26.240 --> 00:43:26.960
You never told me.

00:43:27.520 --> 00:43:29.200
No I didn't, because
I don't do that.

00:43:30.080 --> 00:43:31.360
I just think, good luck.

00:43:32.400 --> 00:43:34.400
But yes, I remember
because Harvey

00:43:34.400 --> 00:43:38.640
was yeah-- he had worked so
hard to try make that happen.

00:43:38.640 --> 00:43:40.240
But we actually pulled
back from doing it

00:43:40.240 --> 00:43:42.720
and I'm quite struck
that you asked

00:43:42.720 --> 00:43:43.920
two questions if I remember.

00:43:43.920 --> 00:43:46.320
One was a challenge, something
you thought would be easy

00:43:46.320 --> 00:43:47.520
and was hard, that was the one.

00:43:48.320 --> 00:43:50.400
And the other thing,
what you thought

00:43:50.400 --> 00:43:53.440
would be hard turned out to be
easy was actually, in a sense,

00:43:53.440 --> 00:43:55.920
getting permission
to go and start

00:43:55.920 --> 00:43:57.120
the new museum in Kendall.

00:43:57.120 --> 00:43:59.760
Because we had given
up, in my time,

00:43:59.760 --> 00:44:01.920
the idea of going
into a new place.

00:44:01.920 --> 00:44:05.120
And then suddenly this
opportunity sprang up.

00:44:05.840 --> 00:44:08.240
And I won't try and
go into the details,

00:44:08.240 --> 00:44:09.840
but suddenly there
was an opportunity

00:44:09.840 --> 00:44:11.200
you never thought was there.

00:44:11.200 --> 00:44:12.880
So that would be the
flip side of the coin.

00:44:13.520 --> 00:44:16.640
I have one that I thought
was going to be difficult

00:44:16.640 --> 00:44:20.640
and then turned easy which
was, around the hacks

00:44:20.640 --> 00:44:21.840
and the collection of hacks.

00:44:22.400 --> 00:44:27.200
And I arrived and I remember
I got a call from someone,

00:44:27.200 --> 00:44:30.800
actually I don't remember
the person's name,

00:44:30.800 --> 00:44:33.200
but I think he was
in material science.

00:44:33.200 --> 00:44:35.840
Anyway, the student newspaper,
which of course I've

00:44:35.840 --> 00:44:38.800
now forgotten the
name of, The Tech

00:44:39.600 --> 00:44:41.520
had done an article
about me arriving.

00:44:41.520 --> 00:44:43.360
And at some point
I had honestly said

00:44:43.360 --> 00:44:45.440
something that led
them to believe

00:44:45.440 --> 00:44:48.320
that I was going to take
the police car off display.

00:44:49.280 --> 00:44:51.280
So the article, the
tenor of the article

00:44:51.280 --> 00:44:53.600
was, who the hell does
this woman think she is?

00:44:54.320 --> 00:44:56.080
She doesn't understand MIT.

00:44:57.920 --> 00:44:59.760
How could she even
conceive of it?

00:44:59.760 --> 00:45:01.760
Which of course, I had
no such conception.

00:45:01.760 --> 00:45:05.360
Anyway, so I he left a
message, I called him back

00:45:05.360 --> 00:45:07.280
and we had a great
conversation and I

00:45:07.280 --> 00:45:09.760
wish I could remember who it was
because then we became friends.

00:45:09.760 --> 00:45:13.760
But I remember thinking
actually inside, oh my God

00:45:13.760 --> 00:45:16.160
how am I going to learn
about this hack collection

00:45:16.160 --> 00:45:18.160
and how are we going to
look after it and whatever.

00:45:18.160 --> 00:45:20.240
And then meeting,
well of course Don

00:45:20.240 --> 00:45:22.080
was around, sorry to
keep mentioning him,

00:45:22.080 --> 00:45:24.320
but around who sort
of was calming me

00:45:24.320 --> 00:45:26.880
down about how, Yes we would
be able to conserve them.

00:45:26.880 --> 00:45:28.880
Because of course, I'm looking
at these things thinking,

00:45:28.880 --> 00:45:30.480
how are we going
to conserve them?

00:45:30.480 --> 00:45:33.120
How are they still going
to be around in 100 years?

00:45:33.120 --> 00:45:35.920
Except for the donuts,
which I don't think ever

00:45:37.200 --> 00:45:39.920
needed conserving because
they just were still exactly

00:45:39.920 --> 00:45:41.920
the same 12 years
after whatever.

00:45:41.920 --> 00:45:44.240
Anyway the donuts never
needed to be replaced.

00:45:45.120 --> 00:45:46.240
They were Dunkin' Donuts.

00:45:46.240 --> 00:45:47.680
They were Dunkin'
Donuts and I was

00:45:47.680 --> 00:45:52.240
like never eat Duncan--
do like 12 years later,

00:45:52.240 --> 00:45:53.600
they still look
exactly the same?

00:45:53.600 --> 00:45:54.880
What is it that they have?

00:45:54.880 --> 00:45:58.080
But then meeting Terry
and just suddenly becoming

00:45:58.080 --> 00:46:00.880
aware that there was this whole
community around the hacks that

00:46:00.880 --> 00:46:03.600
were going to help
me get to grips.

00:46:03.600 --> 00:46:06.320
And suddenly I thought
like I totally get this.

00:46:07.920 --> 00:46:09.840
There are so many
people around to help.

00:46:09.840 --> 00:46:11.600
So that was
something that caused

00:46:11.600 --> 00:46:15.600
me a little inner sort of angst
and then soon became clear

00:46:15.600 --> 00:46:17.920
that everyone at
MIT loved the hacks.

00:46:17.920 --> 00:46:21.840
And so they were going to really
be helpful about doing it.

00:46:21.840 --> 00:46:23.440
So that was something that--

00:46:23.440 --> 00:46:25.840
Speaking of humorous
fun, Warren you

00:46:25.840 --> 00:46:29.040
started something
the Ig Nobel awards.

00:46:30.560 --> 00:46:34.560
And they attracted quite
a bit of attention.

00:46:34.560 --> 00:46:39.200
And my favorite is the year
that they were sponsored by you

00:46:39.200 --> 00:46:42.880
and Cathy and others
invented Kelvin,

00:46:42.880 --> 00:46:47.840
the unisex fragrance that made
all wearers absolutely cool.

00:46:49.520 --> 00:46:52.480
And there were
shirts and t-shirts

00:46:53.120 --> 00:46:57.760
about Kelvin and the fragrance,
but tell maybe people

00:46:57.760 --> 00:46:59.040
about the Ig Nobel awards.

00:46:59.040 --> 00:47:01.120
Many of you have heard
about them at Harvard

00:47:01.120 --> 00:47:02.640
but didn't know
that they actually

00:47:02.640 --> 00:47:04.640
originated here at the museum.

00:47:05.600 --> 00:47:08.960
We were approached by
a student, if we've

00:47:08.960 --> 00:47:10.240
answered in sponsoring it.

00:47:10.800 --> 00:47:18.000
And it sounded like a great
idea so we reserved Kresge,

00:47:18.560 --> 00:47:22.080
and we did I think four
or five years of it

00:47:22.080 --> 00:47:26.080
before it moved up the
street to the other school

00:47:26.080 --> 00:47:27.360
here in Cambridge.

00:47:28.000 --> 00:47:35.680
But at any rate, it was a very
unusual event to say the least.

00:47:36.240 --> 00:47:40.080
I will say in theory,
Kelvin was sold in the shop.

00:47:40.080 --> 00:47:42.320
And we haven't
talked about the shop

00:47:42.320 --> 00:47:44.640
but I know that
the shop has been

00:47:44.640 --> 00:47:52.160
really important to all four of
your tenure here at the museum.

00:47:52.160 --> 00:47:56.160
And I wondered if you have
some memories of getting it--

00:47:56.160 --> 00:47:57.760
how did that get started?

00:47:57.760 --> 00:48:00.800
And the famous
catalog and then Jane,

00:48:00.800 --> 00:48:03.360
you had to-- we had a shop,
then we didn't have a shop,

00:48:03.360 --> 00:48:04.240
then we had a shop.

00:48:04.880 --> 00:48:10.160
Like how did this come to be?

00:48:10.160 --> 00:48:14.160
I do vaguely remember there
had been a previous large store

00:48:14.160 --> 00:48:16.080
that I never saw which
hadn't done so well.

00:48:16.080 --> 00:48:17.120
I recall that.

00:48:17.120 --> 00:48:21.920
Let's just pass discreetly
over that by saying--

00:48:21.920 --> 00:48:24.640
But when I started
we had a tiny store

00:48:24.640 --> 00:48:26.960
and it was a shelf,
or maybe two shelves,

00:48:27.760 --> 00:48:31.440
linked to what was then the
main entrance to this museum

00:48:31.440 --> 00:48:32.480
on the second floor.

00:48:32.480 --> 00:48:36.240
And it was sort of under
where the admissions staff

00:48:36.240 --> 00:48:37.520
person stood.

00:48:37.520 --> 00:48:42.080
And there might have been
20 different items for sale.

00:48:42.080 --> 00:48:44.160
And the amazing
thing was, I still

00:48:44.160 --> 00:48:48.640
remember that tiny store,
not even really a store

00:48:48.640 --> 00:48:51.680
just a couple of shelves,
apparently it was turning over,

00:48:51.680 --> 00:48:54.480
I think, the figure in my
head is $60,000 a year.

00:48:55.360 --> 00:48:57.840
So I mean, that's not much for
a store, but on the other hand,

00:48:58.800 --> 00:49:00.560
there's not much
by way of a store.

00:49:00.560 --> 00:49:02.560
So it's like, well, there
could be something here.

00:49:03.200 --> 00:49:07.120
And so that's why when we opened
this floor, which was in 2007,

00:49:07.760 --> 00:49:11.040
we decided to take a gamble
and bring the store down

00:49:11.040 --> 00:49:14.480
to the ground floor where
it absolutely thrived.

00:49:14.480 --> 00:49:17.120
Well it had a street presence
which it never had before.

00:49:18.240 --> 00:49:20.800
One of the interesting things
about the catalog though,

00:49:20.800 --> 00:49:23.680
is that occurs at
precisely the moment

00:49:23.680 --> 00:49:25.280
that the world is going digital.

00:49:25.280 --> 00:49:30.320
And I'm looking at David Nunez
here and thinking a little bit

00:49:30.320 --> 00:49:33.440
maybe to ask you a little
bit about our digital museum.

00:49:33.440 --> 00:49:35.920
I realize-- who started?

00:49:35.920 --> 00:49:37.600
Who had the first website?

00:49:37.600 --> 00:49:39.520
And when did we--?

00:49:40.560 --> 00:49:43.840
Mary, you were part of getting
the very first experiments

00:49:43.840 --> 00:49:45.520
with getting collections online.

00:49:45.520 --> 00:49:48.880
And Jane, you got a
digitizing holography.

00:49:48.880 --> 00:49:51.760
[INAUDIBLE] Richard's
database that.

00:49:52.560 --> 00:49:53.600
Yes, I remember that.

00:49:53.600 --> 00:49:56.240
But actually we did,
Cara Schneiderman,

00:49:56.240 --> 00:49:59.040
who was our registrar and
collections manager, who's

00:49:59.040 --> 00:50:01.520
now the director of
collections at Jane's museum,

00:50:02.080 --> 00:50:04.800
started the museum
website in the mid 1990's

00:50:04.800 --> 00:50:06.640
when almost nobody
had a website.

00:50:06.640 --> 00:50:09.280
And she did a
fabulous job with it.

00:50:09.280 --> 00:50:13.120
And then really focused on
the Edgerton collection,

00:50:13.120 --> 00:50:17.200
which we had funding to digitize
and make available online.

00:50:17.200 --> 00:50:19.440
So that's kind of I
would say the nucleus

00:50:19.440 --> 00:50:23.920
or the starter collection
for our digital collections.

00:50:25.040 --> 00:50:32.080
I'm interested in a question
about people and audience

00:50:32.080 --> 00:50:33.120
here for the museum.

00:50:33.120 --> 00:50:36.800
And each of you, you've
spoken about alumni

00:50:36.800 --> 00:50:40.320
and we've talked about
people in public.

00:50:40.320 --> 00:50:43.040
But who do you think of
when you first came here,

00:50:43.040 --> 00:50:45.120
what was the target audience?

00:50:45.120 --> 00:50:48.480
And how then have
you viewed it as--

00:50:49.200 --> 00:50:51.120
what have been the
opportunities to expand

00:50:51.120 --> 00:50:53.200
that audience over time?

00:50:53.200 --> 00:50:54.960
I think there's a duality.

00:50:54.960 --> 00:50:58.640
The MIT community is a
very important audience

00:50:58.640 --> 00:51:04.400
but then we as a museum we want
to serve the greater community

00:51:04.400 --> 00:51:04.720
too.

00:51:04.720 --> 00:51:08.560
So I think that figuring
out that mission

00:51:09.200 --> 00:51:13.280
and how to serve the
MIT community and then

00:51:13.280 --> 00:51:14.960
at the same time
the general public.

00:51:15.600 --> 00:51:17.120
I'll just say that as a starter.

00:51:17.920 --> 00:51:19.840
Yeah, no, I think that's right.

00:51:21.040 --> 00:51:25.520
It felt like it was
sort of everybody.

00:51:25.520 --> 00:51:29.840
Which is the same as
saying nobody but yeah,

00:51:29.840 --> 00:51:34.000
I definitely when I came
in felt there was that MIT

00:51:34.000 --> 00:51:35.840
community without question.

00:51:37.040 --> 00:51:40.560
The Cambridge
community and then sort

00:51:40.560 --> 00:51:43.600
of people who were coming
to find out about MIT.

00:51:43.600 --> 00:51:47.440
I mean MIT has this sort
of incredible reputation

00:51:47.440 --> 00:51:48.320
across the world.

00:51:48.320 --> 00:51:50.960
And so there would be
this sense in which

00:51:52.080 --> 00:51:56.320
the museum was the place to
sort of present MIT in all its--

00:51:57.920 --> 00:51:58.960
all the facets.

00:52:00.000 --> 00:52:04.960
And so yeah, I would say
that's how I sort of divided it

00:52:04.960 --> 00:52:06.240
in my head really.

00:52:07.760 --> 00:52:09.760
I don't know if
that's changed a lot.

00:52:09.760 --> 00:52:13.120
I think it is true, just
reflecting on my time,

00:52:13.120 --> 00:52:15.840
that when I came
I think I wanted

00:52:15.840 --> 00:52:20.560
us to give a real emphasis
to audiences outside MIT.

00:52:21.200 --> 00:52:22.800
And the way I used
to put it, so I

00:52:22.800 --> 00:52:23.920
must have believed
this because I

00:52:23.920 --> 00:52:25.200
can remember this little spiel.

00:52:25.840 --> 00:52:31.840
And the spiel was, MIT folks
we really want you on the stage

00:52:33.360 --> 00:52:34.960
more than we want
you in the audience.

00:52:34.960 --> 00:52:36.800
And to me that's
always been the case.

00:52:37.600 --> 00:52:43.520
The MIT faculty and students
are the single most appealing

00:52:43.520 --> 00:52:47.440
exhibits that the MIT
Museum will ever have.

00:52:48.080 --> 00:52:50.240
And my colleagues have
heard me say this before,

00:52:50.240 --> 00:52:54.800
if we have an MIT research
student playing with something

00:52:56.240 --> 00:52:59.760
on the exhibition floor it
will be the star attraction,

00:52:59.760 --> 00:53:00.880
whatever they're doing.

00:53:00.880 --> 00:53:04.160
When we opened this ground
floor, Gary will remember,

00:53:04.160 --> 00:53:07.280
we had an experimental
wheel assembly

00:53:08.720 --> 00:53:11.520
that had been made as part
of the city car project

00:53:11.520 --> 00:53:12.800
by folks in the Media Lab.

00:53:12.800 --> 00:53:16.000
And of course it was
an experimental car.

00:53:16.000 --> 00:53:16.720
So guess what?

00:53:17.360 --> 00:53:20.720
The working wheel assembly
worked for about 12 hours.

00:53:21.680 --> 00:53:22.800
And then it stopped.

00:53:23.600 --> 00:53:26.160
And of course it was a
research student project

00:53:26.160 --> 00:53:28.480
so after a time, and
whenever they felt like it,

00:53:29.040 --> 00:53:30.480
some students turned up.

00:53:30.480 --> 00:53:33.680
They didn't think about
whether there was any audience,

00:53:33.680 --> 00:53:34.320
they just came.

00:53:34.960 --> 00:53:39.680
A bunch of people in jeans and
t-shirts and laptops and they

00:53:39.680 --> 00:53:42.640
started over there
fiddling with this thing

00:53:42.640 --> 00:53:43.840
to try and make it work.

00:53:43.840 --> 00:53:47.280
And before long, the entire,
because the museum was open,

00:53:47.280 --> 00:53:49.520
the entire audience
on the ground floor

00:53:49.520 --> 00:53:51.920
had deserted all
the other exhibits

00:53:51.920 --> 00:53:55.120
and they were all crowded around
these research students who

00:53:55.120 --> 00:53:57.600
were just simply
playing with a laptop

00:53:57.600 --> 00:53:59.360
and doing something, who knows.

00:53:59.360 --> 00:54:01.280
It's always been that
way in my experience.

00:54:01.280 --> 00:54:05.600
So I've always felt that our
greatest asset is the MIT

00:54:05.600 --> 00:54:07.760
community, but we
want to get them

00:54:07.760 --> 00:54:10.320
in front of audiences
who don't get a chance

00:54:10.320 --> 00:54:11.360
to meet them every day.

00:54:11.360 --> 00:54:12.880
So that was what I
was trying to do,

00:54:12.880 --> 00:54:17.200
to swivel our attention as
far as visitors are concerned

00:54:17.200 --> 00:54:19.360
but to do it with the
whole of MIT behind us.

00:54:20.800 --> 00:54:23.600
I'm thinking as we come
to the end of this panel,

00:54:23.600 --> 00:54:27.360
that I was remembering
my first day of work.

00:54:27.360 --> 00:54:29.600
And Jane you called
me into your office

00:54:31.040 --> 00:54:33.120
to discuss your plans for me.

00:54:33.120 --> 00:54:35.760
And they included a
pair of exhibitions.

00:54:35.760 --> 00:54:38.320
One of them was the
aforementioned robotics

00:54:38.320 --> 00:54:40.000
and AI exhibit.

00:54:40.000 --> 00:54:44.720
Another was an exhibit,
and you said to me

00:54:44.720 --> 00:54:46.320
at the time I've
always remembered this,

00:54:46.320 --> 00:54:48.720
robots are to science
museums, what dinosaurs

00:54:48.720 --> 00:54:50.000
are to natural history museums.

00:54:50.000 --> 00:54:54.000
We must do an exhibit and
we come by it honestly here.

00:54:56.640 --> 00:54:58.880
But then you said,
nobody really knows

00:54:58.880 --> 00:55:02.720
what the MIT Museum
has to offer in terms

00:55:02.720 --> 00:55:04.640
of exhibitions and programs.

00:55:04.640 --> 00:55:07.600
But to the extent they
expect anything or have

00:55:07.600 --> 00:55:10.640
any conception, it's that
this will be a place that they

00:55:10.640 --> 00:55:13.200
can learn something about MIT.

00:55:14.000 --> 00:55:18.320
Its history, its achievements,
its challenges, and why MIT

00:55:18.320 --> 00:55:19.200
matters.

00:55:19.200 --> 00:55:22.000
And I thought that might
be a great question

00:55:22.000 --> 00:55:26.400
to pose to you a little bit
about why you think MIT still

00:55:26.400 --> 00:55:27.440
needs a museum.

00:55:27.440 --> 00:55:30.800
And we're about to--
we're at a real inflection

00:55:30.800 --> 00:55:31.920
point in our history.

00:55:31.920 --> 00:55:38.800
We're going to move into
extraordinarily a new facility.

00:55:39.920 --> 00:55:42.720
We have an amazing
new team of people

00:55:42.720 --> 00:55:45.760
here on board to
make that transition

00:55:45.760 --> 00:55:48.320
and I'm really
wondering if you might

00:55:48.320 --> 00:55:52.480
share with us some of your
thoughts about the museum.

00:55:52.480 --> 00:55:54.480
What makes it distinctive?

00:55:54.480 --> 00:55:55.760
Why it's valuable?

00:55:57.600 --> 00:55:59.520
And what you hope
maybe for the future.

00:56:01.360 --> 00:56:04.880
I'm really interested to
hear what you think John.

00:56:04.880 --> 00:56:10.480
I mean to me it seems so
exciting to be a real gateway.

00:56:10.480 --> 00:56:13.360
I mean I think we've always
seen the museum as a gateway

00:56:13.360 --> 00:56:17.280
into MIT but now it's sort of
physically literal, as well

00:56:17.280 --> 00:56:21.920
as sort of in terms
of what we're doing.

00:56:24.000 --> 00:56:26.000
And you know, I'm
tempted to say honestly

00:56:26.000 --> 00:56:28.640
that if MIT didn't
have the museum,

00:56:28.640 --> 00:56:29.840
it would have to reinvent it.

00:56:30.800 --> 00:56:33.920
And so I see it more,
not more, but I see it

00:56:33.920 --> 00:56:36.400
partly in terms of what the
Institute actually needs.

00:56:37.040 --> 00:56:41.280
So MIT is, as we all
know, it's a place devoted

00:56:41.280 --> 00:56:44.320
to knowledge in pursuit
of public service

00:56:44.320 --> 00:56:46.480
and in pursuit of
the public good.

00:56:46.480 --> 00:56:48.640
If you look at the
mission statement for MIT

00:56:48.640 --> 00:56:49.520
that's what it's about.

00:56:50.560 --> 00:56:52.720
And I think we
know enough to know

00:56:52.720 --> 00:56:55.120
that however smart
a group of people

00:56:55.120 --> 00:56:57.600
is, they can't do
that successfully

00:56:57.600 --> 00:57:00.320
if they're doing it in a
closed room on their own.

00:57:00.320 --> 00:57:04.080
They're not going to be able
to get the changes they want

00:57:04.080 --> 00:57:05.520
and make the world
a better place.

00:57:06.080 --> 00:57:08.560
You need to engage the
wider community in that.

00:57:08.560 --> 00:57:13.120
I just think at MIT's work
is so important that it's

00:57:13.120 --> 00:57:16.560
very important that a
wider community be engaged

00:57:16.560 --> 00:57:18.880
in what the Institute is doing.

00:57:18.880 --> 00:57:21.760
And of course, we're not the
only people at MIT who do that.

00:57:21.760 --> 00:57:25.360
But I do think this is a focus
in the Institute for that

00:57:25.360 --> 00:57:26.480
kind of engagement.

00:57:27.120 --> 00:57:28.800
And that's I think
why it's so important.

00:57:28.800 --> 00:57:32.000
I just think it's where we
are in the 21st century,

00:57:32.720 --> 00:57:36.560
we know there's a lot of
questioning of expertise.

00:57:36.560 --> 00:57:39.120
There's a lot of questioning
of science in recent years,

00:57:39.120 --> 00:57:41.600
in ways that some of us
might never have imagined.

00:57:42.240 --> 00:57:44.080
And if we're going to
address those issues

00:57:44.080 --> 00:57:45.040
we've got to be out there.

00:57:45.600 --> 00:57:48.920
And what better place to
be out there from than MIT.

00:57:50.800 --> 00:57:53.760
Well I hope you
all might join me

00:57:53.760 --> 00:57:57.840
in thanking our panelists for
sharing some of their memories

00:57:57.840 --> 00:57:58.400
and thoughts.

00:57:59.200 --> 00:58:01.920
We're going to have an
opportunity to share stories

00:58:01.920 --> 00:58:03.040
with each other.

00:58:03.040 --> 00:58:04.720
In a few moments
our caterers are

00:58:04.720 --> 00:58:08.240
going to bring in
treats for all of us.

00:58:08.240 --> 00:58:14.560
But thank you Warren,
Mary, John, and Jane.

00:58:17.880 --> 00:58:18.880
[LAUGHING]

00:58:18.880 --> 00:58:22.400
This woman had the best
application letter.

00:58:22.400 --> 00:58:23.920
I've hired many people.

00:58:25.440 --> 00:58:29.360
It started, dear, I don't know
whoever, search committee,

00:58:30.640 --> 00:58:35.280
as I sit in my house that
is about 2 and 1/2 million,

00:58:35.280 --> 00:58:39.120
she had the exact number,
smoots from the MIT

00:58:39.120 --> 00:58:39.680
Museum.

00:58:39.680 --> 00:58:43.920
That was the first sentence
and I was like, OK,

00:58:43.920 --> 00:58:45.360
I think this is who we need.

00:58:47.920 --> 00:58:50.560
So yes, I'm the woman that
hired you, my name is Jane.

00:58:53.200 --> 00:58:53.360
[LAUGHING]

00:58:53.360 --> 00:58:55.480
I got out of order
but anyway I want

00:58:55.480 --> 00:58:58.640
to thank you all, both
for today, but also

00:58:58.640 --> 00:59:03.840
for 50 years of amazing
service to this museum.

00:59:03.840 --> 00:59:04.339
[APPLAUSE]

